The text-UI library, the first port of roadmap block 3 (DESIGN D27 sections 8 and 9; docs/d27-c-library.md,
"The text-UI library on Zenix")

What it is: the owner's tui.library, the toolkit of Sumi, Maki and Markdown on AmigaOS (a grid of cells drawn with
escape sequences, only what changed sent; colours, attributes, box lines, keys and their escape codes). On Zenix it
is linked into each program that uses it ($(BUILD)/libtui.a, D27 8: no shared library), over the C library.

upstream/  the owner's source, one source for AmigaOS and Zenix (D27 9, rule 1; owner 2026-10-28), pinned here and
           never changed: the owner's AmigaLibs repository (public, MIT), commit
           cd592016f85fb0859e39f310da039f426eb14734 (2026-10-30), tui.library 0.5b3: src/core.c (the toolkit),
           src/glue.c (the old tui_* names), src/version.h, include/ (libraries/tui.h, clib/tui_protos.h, tui.h)
           and LICENSE (MIT, the notice that travels with any program built from it: tools/mkroot.sh puts it in
           /usr/share/licenses/tui next to /tests/tuitest).
zenix/     Zenix's layer (GPL-2.0-only), kept apart so the copy can be refreshed:
           core.c     compiles upstream/src/core.c unchanged and gives its terminal half on Zenix: termios raw mode
                      (cfmakeraw: ^C and ^Z are keys), poll for every wait, TIOCGWINSZ for the size, a BPTR given
                      to TuiOpen/TuiOpenIO/TuiPossible is a handle, the descriptor + 1 (ports/common's handles; 0 is
                      descriptor 0 for the input, 1 for the output); the upstream terminal functions are renamed by #define and declared static
                      first, so they are compiled but never emitted (the list and the reasons: the file's head)
           glue.c     upstream/src/glue.c as its TUI_STATIC build, tui_possible answered by TuiPossible
           sigfd.c    tui_zenix_sigfd, the descriptor behind a signal bit for TuiWait's extra signals: none here, a
                      member of the library of its own, so a program's own (ports/common/dos.c: its reply ports, a
                      pipe Maki reads) is linked instead when it has one
           include/   amiga.h, the AmigaOS types and the few calls the drawing half makes (Write, AllocVec, FreeVec,
                      CopyMem, CurrentTime) over the C library; the calls only the replaced terminal half makes are
                      declared and defined nowhere (a link error if a kept part ever starts to use one); the
                      exec/, dos/ and proto/ headers the upstream file includes are amiga.h

On Zenix the toolkit's public fields say: CTerm 1 (the console takes the ANSI colours as they are, 256 and 24-bit ones
as the nearest of its 16, and ESC [ ? 25 h/l for the cursor: upstream's CTerm path), UTF8 1 (since the console's
UTF-8, DESIGN D28), CellW and CellH 0. No mouse, no pastes, no second screen (closing clears it). Resize events: a terminal window
(Zenix Desktop's term, a pty) changes size and sends SIGWINCH; the library's handler notes it (a handler the program
had is called too) and ends a wait, and the next TuiWait, TuiPoll or TuiKeyWaiting reads the size again and gives
TE_RESIZE, which the programs answer with TuiResized and a redraw (the AmigaOS layer's Wait answers the terminal's bit
for it). The text console never changes size. TuiSignal answers bit 8 (AmigaOS's SIGF_DOS, where upstream's console packets come
back) and notes the terminal's input descriptor (tui_zenix_tsig, tui_zenix_tfd): the AmigaOS layer's Wait
(ports/common/dos.c, Ahiru's main loop) polls that descriptor for the bit; a program with a poll loop of its own polls
descriptor 0 and calls TuiPoll. TuiWait's extra signals are descriptors (tui_zenix_sigfd names the one behind each
bit): the wait ends with TE_NONE and the bit in *got when it has data, as upstream's extra signals end it.

Widths and UTF-8: the build TUI_UTF8=1 (the Makefile's default since DESIGN D28, the define ZENIX_TUI_UTF8) writes
UTF-8 and takes the widths from the C library's wcwidth (through upstream's own TUI_LIB way to utf8.library's widths,
include/proto/utf8.h: the same tables as the console's); composing accents onto letters is not there. make TUI_UTF8=0
builds the Latin-1 toolkit of before (upstream's stand-ins for a plain console: box lines as - | +, Lodz for the
Polish name); every build compiles the other setting too ($(BUILD)/tui/core-other.o, not linked) so it keeps building.

Found in upstream: a 24-bit colour whose green value had bit 1 set was taken for a pen colour (found here with
0.5b1; fixed upstream in 0.5b2, the 24-bit flag tested first). Since 0.5b3 a cell is 6 bytes, not 16 (its colours and
attributes a number in a table of the styles in use): with a terminal open on 80 x 32 tuitest's process holds
81,920 bytes, not 98,336 (FS-UAE, the stock A1200). The two grids (15,360 bytes each) now come from the allocator's
size classes (16 KB blocks, user/zmalloc.c) instead of a region each, so closing keeps them for the program to use
again rather than giving them back: tools/tui-test.sh checks that a second open and close takes nothing more.

Tests: tools/tui-test.sh (FS-UAE, the stock A1200; in make check): /tests/tuitest (user/tuitest.c) draws its widgets
and reads the console's grid back.

To update: tools/tui-vendor.sh /path/to/AmigaLibs [COMMIT] (the files above from that commit, unchanged), put the
commit here, build, run tools/tui-test.sh; if upstream's terminal half gained a function, add it to zenix/core.c's
list (the link fails if a kept part calls an AmigaOS function amiga.h only declares).
